    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Intermittent issues can be particularly challenging to diagnose. In addition to what I've previously mentioned, consider other potential causes, such as inconspicuous leaks in the cooler lines, which might not be immediately obvious. These could lead to varying fluid levels, causing the transmission to compensate and pressurize unexpectedly. Another factor that can cause these erratic signals or fluid flow is a failing torque converter lockup solenoid, as it might intermittently disrupt transmission fluid flow and pressure during lock-up events. Lastly, an electrical component such as a wiring harness short or loose connection could introduce intermittent electrical noise or communication errors, leading to sporadic pressure changes. Electrical grounds or shielding issues in the transmission may also play a role. A systematic examination eliminating each possibility, possibly with the aid of specialized diagnostic equipment or professional insights, will be necessary to unravel these sudden variations in the hydraulic pressure.",
